        ":question/problem": "Calculate the definite integral $\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{8 x^{2}}{x^{3} + 12}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x^{3} + 12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3 x^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =8 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^{3} + 12.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x^{3} + 12)}\\cdot 8 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{12}^{13}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{12}^{13}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{8}{3}\\int_{12}^{13}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\ln(u)|_{12}^{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(\\ln(13)-\\ln(12)) \\\\  & =- \\frac{8 \\log{\\left(12 \\right)}}{3} + \\frac{8 \\log{\\left(13 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
