        ":question/problem": "Calculate the definite integral $\\int_{1}^{3}\\frac{3 x^{2}}{4 x^{3} + 4}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =4 x^{3} + 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =12 x^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =12 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u & =3 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4 x^{3} + 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 3 \\\\ u & 8 & 112\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{(4 x^{3} + 4)}\\cdot 3 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{8}^{112}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{8}^{112}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{8}^{112}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\ln(u)|_{8}^{112} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\ln(112)-\\ln(8)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\log{\\left(14 \\right)}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
